Hi all,

A failed FTP wildcard `LIST` leaves `data->set.fwrite_func` pointing at libcurl's internal listing parser and `data->set.out` pointing at the easy handle itself, because the code path that restores them is only reached on a *successful* listing. That alone is a crash on reuse. However, `curl_easy_duphandle()` copies this poisoned state verbatim into a clone, so freeing the original handle (an explicitly documented, independent thing to do) turns the clone's next transfer into a heap use-after-free read. A second, adjacent pattern (reusing a handle by only ever changing `CURLOPT_WRITEDATA`, never `CURLOPT_WRITEFUNCTION`) turns it into a type confusion that parses response bytes into a pointer taken from the application's own memory.

## The bug

Wildcard setup saves the application's write callback and output pointer, then substitutes its own:

```c
// lib/ftp.c:4019-4026
/* backup old write_function */
ftpwc->backup.write_function = data->set.fwrite_func;
data->set.fwrite_func = Curl_ftp_parselist;
/* backup old file descriptor */
ftpwc->backup.file_descriptor = data->set.out;
data->set.out = data;
```

The only place that puts them back is the state the machine reaches once the `LIST` response has already parsed successfully:

```c
// lib/ftp.c:4059-4066
case CURLWC_MATCHING: {
  /* In this state is LIST response successfully parsed, so lets restore
     previous WRITEFUNCTION callback and WRITEDATA pointer */
  struct ftp_wc *ftpwc = wildcard->ftpwc;
  data->set.fwrite_func = ftpwc->backup.write_function;
  data->set.out = ftpwc->backup.file_descriptor;
```

Anything that ends the listing before that point (a `550` denial, a dropped data connection, the application's own `CURLOPT_TIMEOUT` firing on a server that just stays silent) skips it. Every teardown path instead runs `Curl_wildcard_dtor()`, called on every transfer completion from `Curl_multi_done()`, and it never sees the `Curl_easy` it would need to restore into:

```c
// lib/ftplistparser.c:188-198
void Curl_wildcard_dtor(struct WildcardData **wcp)
{
  struct WildcardData *wc = *wcp;
  if(!wc) return;
  if(wc->dtor) {
    wc->dtor(wc->ftpwc);   /* frees ftpwc + its parser */
    wc->dtor = ZERO_NULL;
    wc->ftpwc = NULL;
  }
  ...
  curlx_free(wc);
  *wcp = NULL;             /* data->wildcard is now NULL */
```

